      "A woman sits turned three-quarters toward us, hands quietly folded, before a hazy, imaginary landscape of winding rivers and distant peaks. Her faint, ambiguous smile and the soft, smoky modeling of her face — Leonardo's sfumato — make the expression seem to shift as you look.",
    history:
      "Leonardo da Vinci began the portrait around 1503, most likely of Lisa Gherardini, wife of the Florentine merchant Francesco del Giocondo. He never delivered it, kept reworking it for years, and carried it with him to France, where King Francis I acquired it; it has belonged to the French state ever since.\n\nFor centuries it was admired but not uniquely famous — until 1911, when an Italian handyman simply walked out of the Louvre with it hidden under his coat. The two-year manhunt put the picture on front pages around the world and turned it, almost overnight, into a global celebrity.\n\nSince then it has been pelted and sprayed by protesters (always surviving behind its glass), endlessly copied, parodied and merchandised, and treated as the very symbol of what a painting can be. Millions now queue each year for a few seconds before that elusive, sidelong smile.",
