    history:
      "Botticelli painted 'Spring' around 1480 in Florence, an elaborate allegory of love and renewal woven from classical myth. Its exact program is still argued — the coming of spring, Neoplatonic love, or a marriage gift — and no single reading has ever fully settled it.\n\nBotanists have identified some five hundred plants and around two hundred distinct flower species scattered through the grove, most of them blooming in the Tuscan spring, rendered with astonishing botanical care.\n\nRecorded in a Medici townhouse in 1499, it later hung, largely unregarded, in a Medici villa for generations before joining the Birth of Venus in the public spotlight. Like that painting, it owes its modern fame to the 19th-century rediscovery of Botticelli, and the two are now inseparable icons of the age.",
