    history:
      "Raphael frescoed 'The School of Athens' between 1509 and 1511 for the Stanza della Segnatura, a room in the Vatican papal apartments, as part of a scheme celebrating philosophy, theology, poetry and law. It is a showcase of one-point perspective and orderly grouping, all leading the eye to Plato and Aristotle at its vanishing point.\n\nRaphael modeled several of the ancient thinkers on his own contemporaries: Plato is often said to bear the features of Leonardo da Vinci, and the brooding Heraclitus slumped on the steps was added later in the likeness of Michelangelo, then at work nearby on the Sistine ceiling. Raphael even slipped his own quiet self-portrait into the crowd at the right edge.\n\nPainted for a pope while two of the greatest artists in history worked within the same walls, the fresco has come to embody the whole High Renaissance — its confidence, its learning, and its faith that reason and beauty belong together.",
