    history:
      "Jan van Eyck signed and dated the panel in 1434, an early triumph of the new medium of oil paint, whose thin glazes let him build almost hallucinatory detail. Long read as a marriage or betrothal, the scene is dense with possible symbols — the single lit candle, the shed shoes, the dog as an emblem of fidelity — though scholars still debate what, if anything, it actually records.\n\nAbove the convex mirror, whose frame is ringed with tiny scenes of Christ's Passion, Van Eyck wrote 'Johannes de eyck fuit hic' — 'Jan van Eyck was here' — an unusually personal flourish that almost casts the painter and a companion, reflected in the glass, as legal witnesses to the event.\n\nThe panel was later owned by the Spanish crown before vanishing during the Napoleonic Wars and resurfacing with a British officer; it entered the national collection in London in 1842. Its jewel-like realism has made it one of the most analyzed pictures in Western art.",
